(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-atherosclerosis-lipids-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol calculated?","choices":["Low-density plus high-density cholesterol","Total cholesterol minus triglyceride","Triglyceride divided by five","Total minus high-density cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-high-density cholesterol is total cholesterol minus high-density cholesterol, and in hypertriglyceridemia it assesses heart-disease risk better than low-density lipoprotein alone. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"When should a lipid panel be repeated after starting a statin?","choices":["Only after a full year","The next morning","Only if symptoms develop","4 to 12 weeks after starting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Repeat lipids 4 to 12 weeks after starting or adjusting a statin to assess adherence and percentage response, then every 3 to 12 months as needed. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What becomes of very-low-density lipoprotein after it transfers its triglycerides to the cells?","choices":["It becomes high-density lipoprotein in the blood","It is excreted whole into the bile","Its remnants become intermediate-density lipoprotein","It is converted into a chylomicron in the gut"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After the triglyceride is transferred from very-low-density lipoprotein, the remnant particle is intermediate-density lipoprotein, the third of the five classes by density. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"When should most children first receive a non-fasting lipid screen?","choices":["Only after age 20","Only if a xanthoma appears","Between ages 9 and 11","At birth for every infant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pediatric patients get a non-fasting lipid profile once between ages 9 and 11 and again later in adolescence; elective screening can start at 2 with a family history. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How is low high-density lipoprotein best improved?","choices":["Lifestyle modification","Bile acid sequestrants","Anabolic steroids","Beta blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Improving high-density lipoprotein is best done by lifestyle: smoking cessation (up to 10%), weight loss (about 1 mg/dL per 6 pounds) and frequent aerobic exercise (about 5%). — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which newer lipid drug is taken by mouth?","choices":["Evinacumab","Evolocumab","Bempedoic acid","Inclisiran"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bempedoic acid is administered orally, whereas evinacumab is intravenous, inclisiran is subcutaneous, and evolocumab and alirocumab are injectable monoclonal antibodies. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What is the critical first step in managing dyslipidemia?","choices":["Determine which lipoproteins are raised or lowered","Start a high-intensity statin in every patient","Begin a fibrate to lower triglyceride first","Refer to cardiology before any lipid testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The critical first step is determining which lipoproteins are decreased or increased, and the primary target of cholesterol-lowering therapy is elevated low-density lipoprotein cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What are the two broad causes of dyslipidemia?","choices":["Hepatic and renal failure only","Primary genetic and secondary causes","Infectious and autoimmune causes","Dietary fat and dietary sugar only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dyslipidemias are the result of primary (genetic) causes and secondary causes, which are lifestyle, medical conditions and medications. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which lipid is the better predictor of heart disease?","choices":["Chylomicron concentration","Low-density lipoprotein cholesterol","Serum triglyceride level","Very-low-density lipoprotein level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk of atherosclerosis is directly related to raised serum cholesterol, and elevated low-density lipoprotein cholesterol is the primary target of cholesterol-lowering therapy. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes lipoprotein(a) from low-density lipoprotein?","choices":["It carries apolipoprotein B-48 instead of B-100","It is mainly triglyceride rather than cholesterol","It is made in the intestine rather than the liver","It carries apolipoprotein(a) as well as B-100"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lipoprotein(a) is similar to low-density lipoprotein in lipid and protein composition but carries a distinct protein, apolipoprotein(a), in addition to apolipoprotein B-100. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"About how much does a high-intensity statin lower low-density lipoprotein?","choices":["About 50% or more","About 30% to 49%","Under 30%","About 10% to 15%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High intensity lowers low-density lipoprotein about 50% or more, moderate intensity about 30% to under 50%, and low intensity under 30%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Before starting a statin for primary prevention at age 40 to 75, what is recommended?","choices":["A mandatory calcium score","A clinician-patient risk discussion","A skin biopsy for receptor activity","A trial of a fibrate first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The discussion reviews major risk factors, 10-year risk, risk enhancers, benefits of lifestyle and statins, adverse effects and interactions, cost, and patient preferences in shared decision-making. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which drug raises high-density lipoprotein more than any other lipid medication?","choices":["Niacin","Ezetimibe","Colestipol","Atorvastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises high-density lipoprotein 15% to 35%, better than other medications, and also lowers triglyceride 20% to 50% and low-density lipoprotein 5% to 25%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"In primary prevention at intermediate 10-year risk, what favors starting a statin?","choices":["A calcium score of zero","A high-density lipoprotein of 65","Age under 40","Risk-enhancing factors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At intermediate risk (7.5% to 19.9%) in adults 40 to 75 without diabetes, risk-enhancing factors favor starting a statin, which then aims to lower low-density lipoprotein by 30% or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which conditions must be ruled out before a statin when low-density lipoprotein is elevated?","choices":["Hypothyroidism and nephrotic syndrome","Pregnancy and chronic kidney disease","Cigarette smoking and obesity","Pancreatitis and gouty arthritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With elevated low-density lipoprotein rule out hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, primary biliary cirrhosis and anorexia nervosa before starting a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which acute complication does severe hypertriglyceridemia put a patient at risk of?","choices":["Sudden death in childhood","Acute pancreatitis","Tendon rupture","Acute kidney injury"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 mg/dL carries a risk of acute pancreatitis; above 500 the plan is a very low-fat diet, weight management, activity and a fibrate, niacin or both. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A low-density lipoprotein cholesterol of 165 mg/dL falls in which category?","choices":["Borderline high","Very high","Near optimal","High"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The bands are under 100 optimal, 100 to 129 near or above optimal, 130 to 159 borderline high, 160 to 189 high, and above 190 very high. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A fasting triglyceride of 620 mg/dL falls in which category?","choices":["High","Borderline high","Normal","Very high"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride is normal under 150, borderline high at 150 to 199, high at 200 to 499 and very high above 500 mg/dL, the range that carries the risk of acute pancreatitis. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"For a patient older than 75 with clinical atherosclerotic disease, what is reasonable?","choices":["Lifestyle alone, without any drug therapy","Starting a moderate- or high-intensity statin","Stopping all statin therapy at age 75","Adding a fibrate in place of a statin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Above 75, initiating a moderate- or high-intensity statin is reasonable, and continuing a high-intensity statin already in use is also reasonable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which endocrine disorder should be screened for in a patient with elevated low-density lipoprotein?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Adrenal insufficiency","Hyperparathyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low thyroid hormone down-regulates hepatic low-density lipoprotein receptors and raises low-density lipoprotein, so patients with elevated levels should be screened for low thyroid. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What fundoscopic finding is seen in familial chylomicronemia syndrome?","choices":["Xanthelasma","Papilledema","Lipemia retinalis","Corneal arcus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lipemia retinalis is opalescence of the retinal blood vessels, seen with fasting triglyceride above 500 and usually above 1000 mg/dL, alongside eruptive xanthomas and pancreatitis. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the role of high-density lipoprotein in reverse cholesterol transport?","choices":["Carries dietary triglyceride from the gut to muscle","Moves triglyceride made in the liver out to the cells","Carries excess cholesterol from the periphery to the liver","Delivers cholesterol to cells for their own needs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-density lipoprotein takes excess cholesterol from the periphery back to the liver for excretion into the bile, which is why it is the 'good' cholesterol and carries decreased risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which apolipoprotein is the major structural protein of high-density lipoprotein?","choices":["Apolipoprotein(a)","Apolipoprotein A-I","Apolipoprotein B-100","Apolipoprotein E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apolipoprotein A-I is made by the liver and intestines, is the major structural protein of high-density lipoprotein and is found on every high-density particle. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which drug class is the most effective for lowering triglycerides?","choices":["Ezetimibe","Fibrates","Statins","Bile acid sequestrants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates such as gemfibrozil and fenofibrate are the most effective for lowering triglyceride, 20% to 50%, and also raise high-density lipoprotein 10% to 30%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"How does homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia usually present?","choices":["In infancy, with pancreatitis and eruptive papules","In old age, with corneal arcus alone","In childhood, with xanthomas and early atherosclerosis","In middle age, found on routine screening"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The homozygous form presents in childhood with cutaneous xanthomas on the hands, wrists, elbows, knees, heels or buttocks and symptomatic atherosclerosis before puberty. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What does corneal arcus before age 40 usually indicate?","choices":["Recent acute pancreatitis","A genetic lipid disorder","Normal aging of the eye","Severe hypertriglyceridemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corneal arcus is a white or gray ring around the cornea from cholesterol deposition; common in older adults (arcus senilis), it usually indicates an underlying genetic condition before 40. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which physical-activity target is part of therapeutic lifestyle changes?","choices":["Low intensity, once a week","Complete rest until lipids improve","Moderate intensity, 30 minutes a day","High intensity, 5 minutes a day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Increased physical activity means moderate intensity for 30 minutes per day, such as brisk walking, biking or active yoga. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"How does a high-density lipoprotein of 60 mg/dL or more affect the coronary risk-factor count?","choices":["It adds one risk factor","It doubles the calculated risk","It has no effect on the count","It removes one risk factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-density lipoprotein of 60 mg/dL or more counts as a negative risk factor, so its presence removes one risk factor from the total; below 40 mg/dL it counts as a risk factor. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which measurement defines abdominal obesity for metabolic syndrome?","choices":["Waist circumference","Body mass index","Total body weight","Hip circumference"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Abdominal obesity is defined by waist circumference, more than 40 inches in men and more than 35 inches in women, rather than by body weight or body mass index. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which skin finding is distinctive of familial dysbetalipoproteinemia?","choices":["Palmar xanthomas","Xanthelasma of the eyelids","Achilles tendon xanthomas","Corneal arcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Its cutaneous xanthomas are distinctive: palmar xanthomas and tuberoeruptive xanthomas, and the disorder is associated with accelerated atherosclerosis. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 56"}]};
